From: BerryMC <BerryMC@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 17 Nov 2026 01:40:15 +0800
Subject: [PATCH] Optimize scheduled tick processing

Optimizations to the WorldServer NextTickListEntry processing loop
(WorldServer.a(boolean)), behind scheduled-ticks.optimize (default
false):

- Drain due entries from the HashTreeSet with a new pollFirst()
  (single TreeSet operation + hash removal) instead of the
  first() + remove() double lookup per entry. The not-yet-due entry
  is re-added on break, preserving ordering since the set is ordered
  by due time.
- Hoist the worldData.getTime() lookup out of the drain loop.
- Pass the entry position directly to areChunksLoadedBetween instead
  of allocating two throwaway BlockPosition.a(0,0,0) instances per
  entry; both are coordinate-identical so the semantics are
  unchanged.

Redstone-heavy worlds schedule thousands of entries per tick and walk
this loop constantly.

diff --git a/src/main/java/dev/pomf/dionysus/DionysusConfig.java b/src/main/java/dev/pomf/dionysus/DionysusConfig.java
index 57793ecbaa3a03cbbb82a1c96060ce231d1853d4..7d90569b0393b9cdef862300ff64672a45b7f5e4 100644
--- a/src/main/java/dev/pomf/dionysus/DionysusConfig.java
+++ b/src/main/java/dev/pomf/dionysus/DionysusConfig.java
@@ -443,4 +443,15 @@ public class DionysusConfig {
                 The radius in blocks in which a player must be to allow pathfinding
                 when pathfinding.optimize is enabled.""");
     }
+
+    public static boolean optimizeScheduledTicks = false;
+    private static void optimizeScheduledTicks() {
+        optimizeScheduledTicks = getBoolean("scheduled-ticks.optimize", optimizeScheduledTicks, """
+                Optimize the WorldServer scheduled tick (NextTickListEntry) processing
+                loop: drain due entries with a single pollFirst() per entry instead of
+                first() + remove() double lookups, hoist the time lookup out of the
+                loop and avoid per-entry BlockPosition allocations. Redstone-heavy
+                worlds schedule thousands of ticks per tick and pay this cost
+                constantly.""");
+    }
 }
